# Conclusion

The application was vulnerable to an **HTTP Header-Based Access Control Bypass** because different infrastructure components interpreted the request differently.

The reverse proxy authorized the request based on the visible URL, while the application executed the request using the value contained in the `X-Original-URL` header.

This discrepancy enabled an attacker to bypass administrative restrictions and invoke privileged functionality without authentication.

---

## Key Takeaways

- Never trust client-controlled routing headers.
- Reverse proxies and applications must authorize the same effective resource.
- Internal infrastructure headers should be stripped from external requests.
- Authorization must occur after URL rewriting.
- Inconsistent request interpretation is a common source of Broken Access Control vulnerabilities.

